# Cost controls for agentic tools: why alerts and capacity are not brakes

**Level: Intermediate**

Agentic systems can make repeated model calls, invoke tools, retry failures, delegate work, and continue until a goal or stopping condition is reached. This makes their cost profile different from that of a single API request.

This module is written for intermediate learners who are familiar with basic concepts such as agentic execution graphs, fan-out, episodes, and admission control. If you are not familiar with these terms, you may need to review foundational material before proceeding.

A dashboard can show that spending is high. An alert can notify someone. A concurrency limit can slow the system down. None of these mechanisms necessarily stops further spending.

To enforce a cost limit, the system needs a control in the execution path that can refuse new work.

## Learning objectives

After completing this module, you should be able to:

- distinguish alerts, capacity controls, and cost brakes;
- explain why agentic execution can overshoot a budget;
- estimate the possible overshoot after an alert;
- design admission control using budget reservations;
- place limits at the agent, workflow, tenant, and account levels;
- test whether a claimed spending limit is actually enforceable.

## 1. Three controls that solve different problems

The terms *budget*, *quota*, and *limit* are often used loosely. Start by classifying a control according to what it actually does.

| Control | Primary purpose | Typical response at threshold | Does it guarantee a spending ceiling? |
|---|---|---|---|
| Alert | Visibility and notification | Sends a message or creates an event | No |
| Capacity control | Limits simultaneous or per-period resource use | Delays or rejects work when capacity is exhausted | Usually no |
| Cost brake | Prevents additional financial liability | Rejects, cancels, degrades, or requires approval before work proceeds | Yes, if coverage and metering are sound 

... (truncated — full content in delivery/private/local-proposals/)

</details>

