# How a Model You Host Differs from a Subscription or a Vendor Key

The important distinction is not **which model name appears in the interface**. It is **who operates the inference service, who controls the surrounding system, and who carries the operational risk**.

A model offered through a subscription, the same or a similar model called through an API key, and a model deployed on your infrastructure can produce comparable text while being fundamentally different systems.

---

## The Three Access Patterns

| Access Pattern          | What You Receive                          | Who Runs Inference       | Typical Billing Unit       | Your Main Responsibility                     |
|-------------------------|-------------------------------------------|--------------------------|----------------------------|-----------------------------------------------|
| Application subscription| Access to a provider’s user-facing tool   | Provider                 | User, seat, or time period | Account administration and acceptable use     |
| Vendor API key          | A credential for programmatic access      | Provider                 | Usage, capacity, or allowance | Application integration and credential security |
| Model you host          | Model artifacts under a license           | You or your operator     | Compute, storage, operations | The complete inference service                |

These categories describe responsibilities, not commercial labels. A vendor may call an API plan a “subscription,” and a hosted offering may include managed operations. Read the service description and contract to determine who controls what.

---

## 1. An Application Subscription is Access to a Finished Tool

A subscription gives people access to a provider-controlled interface. The provider chooses and operates the model, runtime, safety controls, update schedule, and user experience.

A subscription does **not** give you:

- programmatic access to the model;
- a transferable allowance

... (truncated — full content in delivery/private/local-proposals/)

</details>

