# Mapping our orchestration patterns onto named frameworks
**Field Guide – Intermediate Level**

---

## How to map observable behavior to named patterns

Use this table to classify an orchestration design based on its behavior, not on its diagrams, class names, or vendor terminology.

| Observable behavior | Primary named framework | Additional applicable pattern | Do not call it this when… |
|---|---|---|---|
| A central component decides which participant acts next and tracks progress | **Process Manager** | Orchestrated Saga, state machine | The component only forwards messages without tracking process state |
| Participants react to events and publish subsequent events without a central controller | **Choreography** | Event-driven Saga, publish–subscribe | A hidden coordinator still determines the sequence |
| A business operation spans multiple independently committed changes and uses compensating actions | **Saga** | Process Manager or choreography | The operation is one local atomic transaction, or failure is handled only by retry |
| A message carries the sequence of destinations it should visit | **Routing Slip** | Dynamic routing | A coordinator keeps the route and sends each command itself |
| One request is sent to several handlers and their replies are collected | **Scatter–Gather** | Aggregator, correlation identifier | Results are never collected or evaluated together |
| Progress is represented as states and permitted transitions | **Finite-state machine** or **statechart** | Process Manager | The “states” are merely log messages or task statuses with no transition rules |
| Tasks run when their predecessors are complete, with no cycles allowed | **DAG workflow** | Dataflow or dependency scheduling | The process can intentionally return to an earlier task |
| A process includes events, gateways, human work, timers, and explicit end states | **BPMN process** | Saga or state machine | The diagram is only informal boxes and arrows |
| Data passes throu

... (truncated — full content in delivery/private/local-proposals/)

</details>

